{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310854,"nonce":"N4+VD6vkfNPvoHmg5GirQlKCR8I=","title":"Authoritative list of compiler-level work the nue ML framework needs landed before its kernels can express themselves without workarounds. ...","message":"`rayzor.concurrent.NumaPool` (Phase 1b-ii, 2026-05-18) shipped with the multi-node fanout deliberately stubbed and its API expressed in workaround syntax (curried function types instead of parameterised). Most of the cost is paid in `parallelFor` / `parallelRows`, but Phase 3+ kernels (matmul, attention, conv) hit the same set of issues at scale. This file enumerates the compiler fixes — **none of these need to be solved by adding new hacks**; they all have well-defined fixes inside the existing compiler.\n\nResolve before Phase 3 needs real fanout (probably the natural order to land them is the same as their numbering below).\n\n\n**Resolved 2026-05-19 (commit pending):** Root cause was `parse_function_decl` in `parser/src/rd/decls.rs` not consuming the trailing `;` of expression-body declarations (`function f():T return expr;`). The leftover `;` made `parse_class_body` emit a parse error, which made the RD parser return Err, which made the nom fallback run, which silently produced an empty file for syntax nom didn't understand (parameterised arrow types). Fix: have `parse_function_decl` `eat(Semicolon)` after parsing the expression body. Phase 1c multi-node fanout works now — `examples/numa-pool/Main.hx` exercises real `Thread.spawn` per NUMA node and produces correctly-partitioned output. Regression test: `compiler/tests/haxe/test_parser_expression_body_terminator.hx`.\n\n\n**Resolved 2026-05-19:** root cause was lexer-level — `\u003e\u003e` tokenises as `TokenKind::Shr` and `\u003e\u003e\u003e` as `TokenKind::Ushr`, but `parse_type_param_args` / `parse_type_args` / `parse_type_params` only consumed `Gt` to close. When a class contained `Array\u003cThread\u003cInt\u003e\u003e` (and similar nested generics), the parser bailed with `expected type, found '\u003e\u003e'`, the rd parser returned Err, the nom fallback ran, and (combined with the file containing newer syntax nom didn't know like parameterised arrow types) silently dropped the class declaration entirely.\n\n**Fix:** `TokenStream` now exposes `expect_closing_gt` and `at_closing_gt`. The former virtually splits a `Shr` into 2 `Gt` slots and a `Ushr` into 3 `Gt` slots — each call to `expect_closing_gt` claims one slot, and `advance` only moves past the underlying token when all slots are consumed. `at_closing_gt` returns true at `Gt`, `Shr`, or `Ushr` so the loop terminates at the right spot.\n\nAll three close-Gt sites updated:\n- `parse_type_param_args` (e.g. `Array\u003cT\u003e` in type position)\n- `parse_type_args` (e.g. `new Array\u003cT\u003e(...)` in expression position)\n- `parse_type_params` (e.g. `\u003cT, U\u003e` in class declarations)\n\n**Verified end-to-end:** `examples/numa-pool/Main.hx` now runs the FULL NumaPool with the readable `fn:(idx:Int, node:Int)-\u003eVoid` signatures + `Thread\u003cInt\u003e` generics + multi-node fanout. Regression test: `compiler/tests/haxe/test_parser_nested_generic_close.hx` covers `Array\u003cInt\u003e` (single), `Array\u003cArray\u003cInt\u003e\u003e` (double, Shr), `Array\u003cArray\u003cArray\u003cInt\u003e\u003e\u003e` (triple, Ushr), and the parameterised-arrow + Thread\u003cT\u003e combo. 709/709 lib tests pass.\n\n**Minimal repro (`/tmp/test_param/rayzor/concurrent/MyPool.hx`):**\n```haxe\npackage rayzor.concurrent;\nclass MyPool {\n    private var _nodeCount:Int;\n    public function new() { _nodeCount = NumaTopology.nodeCount(); }\n    public function count():Int return 7;                     // ← removing this fixes it\n    public function parallelFor(items:Int, fn:(idx:Int, node:Int)-\u003eVoid):Void {\n        var f = fn;\n        var t = Thread.spawn(function():Int { f(0, 0); return 0; });\n        t.join();\n    }\n    private static var _instance:MyPool;\n    public static function global():MyPool { if (_instance == null) _instance = new MyPool(); return _instance; }\n}\n```\n\n`Main.hx` calling `MyPool.global()` then fails with `E0100: Cannot access field 'global': class not registered or field does not exist`. Removing the `count()` method (any extra instance method, even one returning a literal) makes everything work.\n\n**What I've ruled out by bisection:**\n- Single method with parameterised arrow type + sibling method: works (so it's not fix #2 alone)\n- Single method with Thread.spawn capturing `fn` + sibling method: works (not fix #1 alone)\n- The interaction with `NumaTopology.nodeCount()` extern call: doesn't matter — removing it doesn't help\n- Method name `nodeCount` vs `count`: doesn't matter — same failure either way\n- Field access inside `count()` vs literal return: doesn't matter\n\n**Suspected location:** TAST lowering of the lambda inside `Thread.spawn` — when the lambda captures a Function value AND the enclosing class has multiple instance methods, something in the class-field-table population aborts silently, leaving the class unregistered. Probably `compiler/src/tast/ast_lowering.rs` around class member iteration or `compute_captures`-driven type lookup.\n\n**Workaround for NumaPool today:** keep `parallelFor` inline-only (no `Thread.spawn` body). Multi-node fanout remains the v1.5 promise. The example demo at `examples/numa-pool/Main.hx` runs cleanly with the inline-only version.\n\n**Fix priority:** HIGH — without it, NUMA fanout is permanently inline.\n\n---\n\n\n**Blocks:** `parallelFor` / `parallelRows` actually fanning out via `Thread.spawn`. Every parallel kernel (matmul rows, attention heads, conv tiles, elementwise sweeps) depends on this.\n\n**Where:** [`compiler/src/tast/trait_checker.rs:110-111`](compiler/src/tast/trait_checker.rs#L110-L111) —\n```rust\n// Function types: NOT Send/Sync by default (captures unknown)\nTypeKind::Function { .. } =\u003e false,\n```\n\n**Why it's wrong:** A bare function pointer with no captures is bit-pattern Send (it's a code address — identical to a primitive). The current rule is over-conservative — it conflates \"function value at API boundary\" with \"closure that might have non-Send captures.\"\n\n**Clean fix:** Make `TypeKind::Function` Send unconditionally. The existing `validate_capture_is_send` ([send_sync_validator.rs:167-187](compiler/src/tast/send_sync_validator.rs#L167-L187)) is already invoked at every `Thread.spawn` and rejects closures whose captures are non-Send. So when a function VALUE crosses the API boundary as a parameter, it's just an opaque ptr (Send). When that value is later spawned, the captures are checked at the spawn site — separately, properly.\n\n**Once landed:** NumaPool can spawn one worker per NUMA node, each pinned via `NumaTopology.bindCurrent(node)`, executing the user's `fn`. No API changes — `parallelFor` / `parallelRows` signatures stay the same; only the body grows.\n\n\n**Blocks:** Readable function-type signatures in stdlib + nue classes. Currently `NumaPool.parallelFor` writes `fn:Int-\u003eInt-\u003eVoid` instead of the much clearer `fn:(idx:Int, node:Int)-\u003eVoid`.\n\n**Where:** TAST lowering of class field signatures. Likely [`compiler/src/tast/ast_lowering.rs`](compiler/src/tast/ast_lowering.rs) — the parser accepts the syntax, the TAST loses it. Bug-tracked in `bugs_known.md`.\n\n**Symptom:** A pure-Haxe class with any method having a `(name:T, name:T)-\u003eR` typed parameter is silently NOT registered. Consumer code fails with `E0100: class not registered or field does not exist` on **every** access (not just the function-typed field).\n\n**Clean fix:** Make TAST lowering preserve parameter-named arrow function types in class field signatures. Failing that, emit a proper parse error so the silent invalidation becomes a loud one — at minimum the failure mode must surface.\n\n**Once landed:** Switch NumaPool signatures back to `fn:(idx:Int, node:Int)-\u003eVoid` and `fn:(rowStart:Int, rowEnd:Int, node:Int)-\u003eVoid`. Every nue layer (Linear, Embedding, Attention) that uses callbacks benefits.\n\n\n**Blocks:** Exposing `rayzor_numa_node_cpus` (and any future API that fills a caller-allocated buffer) directly to Haxe. Currently the `Ptr` type token in `declare_native_methods!` has no Haxe-side counterpart, so `node_cpus` is registered as a C symbol but dropped from the method descriptor table.\n\n**Where:** The Haxe stdlib needs a `Ptr\u003cT\u003e` (or `Buffer\u003cT\u003e`) abstract that maps cleanly to `*mut T` for FFI. Other rayzor APIs that need this: `runtime/src/haxe_array.rs` (the data ptr accessors), tensor `data():Ptr\u003cFloat\u003e`, future GGUF mmap reads.\n\n**Clean fix:** Add `rayzor.Ptr\u003cT\u003e` extern with `alloc(numel)`, `free()`, indexer ops, and a `Ptr\u003cInt\u003e`-compatible Haxe-side helper that wraps an `Array\u003cInt\u003e` for the common case. Then `declare_native_methods!` parameters typed `Ptr` map to it.\n\n**Once landed:** Haxe wrappers can express APIs that return arrays via output buffers (the C-idiomatic pattern) — `NumaTopology.nodeCpus(node):Array\u003cInt\u003e` becomes an idiomatic one-liner over the existing C symbol.\n\n\n**Blocks:** Reduction-style parallel APIs — `parallelFor\u003cT\u003e(items, fn:Int-\u003eT):T` where the pool calls `fn` per chunk and combines partial T results. Sum reductions, max-finding, dot products all want this.\n\n**Where:** `Thread.spawn\u003cT\u003e(fn:Void-\u003eT):Thread\u003cT\u003e` already declares the generic, but the rayzor JIT codegen for closures captures `Void-\u003ei64` (line 253 of `concurrency.rs`). Closure return types are erased to `i64` post-codegen.\n\n**Clean fix:** Either preserve the generic return type through MIR lowering (large), or accept the i64-erasure for now and add a typed `Thread.spawn_int / spawn_ptr / spawn_float` family that the Haxe macro layer dispatches over at the call site. The macro path lets us keep the generic Haxe API while the runtime stays untouched.\n\n**Once landed:** NumaPool grows `reduce\u003cT\u003e(items, init:T, fn:(idx:Int, acc:T)-\u003eT):T` — the canonical reduction primitive ML kernels need.\n\n\n**Blocks:** Persistent worker pool (the v1.5 NumaPool) — workers loop on a Channel waiting for work, exit on a close signal. Without a clean shutdown contract, the pool either leaks threads or has to hand-roll close handshakes.\n\n**Where:** `rayzor.concurrent.Channel` has `close()` but the semantics for receivers-pending-on-close aren't part of the public contract. Want: `Channel.close()` causes pending `receive()` to return a \"channel closed\" sentinel (Option-like or explicit method).\n\n**Clean fix:** Document Channel close semantics + add a `tryReceive():Option\u003cT\u003e` or `recvOrClose():ReceiveResult\u003cT\u003e` that distinguishes \"channel closed, no more values\" from \"empty but open.\"\n\n**Once landed:** NumaPool can ship the pooled fast-path — workers spin on `recvOrClose`, exit cleanly when the pool is destroyed (or process-end finalizes the singleton).\n\n\n`global()` singletons need a private static var + null check pattern. Adding `@:singleton` metadata that auto-generates the boilerplate is a QoL win but not a blocker. Defer.\n\n---\n\n\nAdd `compiler/tests/haxe/test_numa_parallel_for.hx`:\n- Spawn-thread-id-per-iteration check (each chunk runs on a different OS thread)\n- Multi-socket matmul benchmark (manual, CI hardware doesn't have NUMA)\n- Send-validation negative test (a closure capturing a non-Send var is rejected at parallelFor's spawn site, not silently)\n\nAdd `compiler/tests/haxe/test_numa_pool_reduction.hx` after item 4 lands.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_numa_followups` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}